Pidgeoncide
-----------

[![CI](https://github.com/arximboldi/pigeoncide/actions/workflows/ci.yaml/badge.svg)](https://github.com/arximboldi/pigeoncide/actions/workflows/ci.yaml)

Pidgeoncide is a funny game where you control an ill-minded child
whose imaginary friend tells him to kill all the pidgeons in the
world.

![image](https://sinusoid.es/static/screens/pigeoncide.png)

Usage
-----

The easiest way to play is with [Nix](https://nixos.org) (flakes
enabled) on x86_64 or aarch64 Linux:

> ```
> nix run github:arximboldi/pigeoncide
> ```

On Linux distributions other than NixOS, Nix-built programs cannot see
the system's OpenGL drivers; run it through
[nixGL](https://github.com/nix-community/nixGL) instead.

From a checkout, `nix run` starts the game, `nix flake check` runs
the unit tests, and `nix develop` opens a shell with Python 3.13 and
Panda3D where you can use the scripts directly:

> ```
> ./pigeoncide        # play
> ./pigeoncide -s game  # skip the menu and start a level
> ./unittests         # run the unit tests
> ```

Without Nix you need Python 3 and Panda3D 1.10 (`pip install
panda3d`); then run `./pigeoncide`.

Controls
--------

| Key          | Action                                              |
|--------------|-----------------------------------------------------|
| W A S D      | Move and strafe                                     |
| Mouse, K L   | Turn                                                |
| C (hold)     | Run                                                 |
| Space        | Jump                                                |
| E            | Hit with the weapon in hand                         |
| R            | Throw the weapon away                               |
| F            | Drop food to lure the pigeons                       |
| Q            | Place a stick; two sticks close together make a laser fence |
| Escape       | Pause menu                                          |

The keys can be changed in the options menu.

Web version
-----------

The game also builds for web browsers, compiled to WebAssembly:

> ```
> nix build .#pigeoncide-web   # static site in ./result
> nix run .#serve-web          # try it at http://localhost:8000/
> nix run .#serve-web -- 8001  # or on another port
> ```

CI builds the site for every pull request and push to master too:
download the `pigeoncide-web` artifact from a run's page to get the
same four files.

To publish it, upload the four files in `result` (`index.html`,
`pigeoncide.js`, `pigeoncide.wasm` and `pigeoncide.data`) to any
static web host. They are large (about 60 MB), so enable gzip or
Brotli compression on the server if it can. The page needs a browser
with WebGL 2.

The web build uses Panda3D's WebGL port, which is not released yet,
from a pinned commit of Panda3D's master branch, with two small fixes
in `nix/packages/panda3d-wasm`. The first build compiles CPython and
Panda3D to WebAssembly and takes several minutes.

The level model
---------------

The level's grass is mapped by `tools/retexture.py`, which gives the
grass the same scale everywhere. To change that scale, run it again
with a different number of world units per grass tile (100 now):

> ```
> nix develop -c python3 tools/retexture.py data/lvl/world.egg data/lvl/world.egg 100
> ```

For more information, visit the **project page** at:
https://savannah.nongnu.org/projects/pigeoncide/
